    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_8a675c46403ac3a0fd330ef41ab5b516389e9c8b27b94874623b87fa0a8f58fb",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The sources give two exact ways to audit a formal moment expansion. A Voronoi-series calculation fails because bounded convergence on fixed finite intervals does not justify integration over an improper range, and the repair inserts the cutoff before substitution. A long-polynomial second-moment calculation requires a Fourier identity, proved using the zeta functional equation, to reconcile off-diagonal coefficient mass with the classical mean square; for higher moments, a negative predicted value is an exact sign contradiction showing that the heuristic is incomplete.",
